# Core Coding Standards

- Prefer the smallest change that solves the problem clearly.
- Understand why a line, helper, dependency, or abstraction is needed before adding it.
- Remove redundant, unused, or dead code when it is part of the problem being solved.
- Follow the style, naming, and structure already used near the code being edited.
- Avoid broad refactors unless they directly support the requested change.
- Keep generated output and explanations concise.
- Add tests or checks when behavior changes, and keep them focused on the changed behavior.

```cpp
// Avoid keeping unused variables, commented-out branches, or duplicate logic.
// Prefer one clear implementation that matches the surrounding code.
```
